          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "764abdf6-5f19-3ec8-9cb5-3d67d475cf41",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use secure alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `vsnprintf`, you can use `snprintf` with a maximum size limit. Also, always control the format string and never pass user input directly to it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n\nvoid safe_printf(const char *format, ...)\n{\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    char buffer[256];\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `vsnprintf` is used safely by specifying a maximum size for the output string. The format string is controlled by the programmer and not influenced by user input.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the following libraries:\n\n- stdio.h\n- stdarg.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d055809-a5e6-387f-b778-0cc740ca8277",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c401d32a-a313-3a76-8d7b-a8783ceb19de",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94a3e768-5f18-3649-b377-aad770f71284",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `vsprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the output buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if the input is larger than the buffer size.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking. For example, instead of `vsprintf`, use `vsnprintf`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the size of the output buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsprintf` function with `vsnprintf`:\n\n```cpp\nvsnprintf(&str[0], sizeof(str), format, va);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "98d77baa-ebfb-3680-b50e-c03096f441b8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen()` function in C++. The `strlen()` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. This is because `strlen()` does not include the null terminator (`\\0`) in its count, which can lead to off-by-one errors. In the provided code snippet, the `strlen(tag)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `tag` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that include the null terminator in their count, such as `strnlen()`. Additionally, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated to prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nstrncmp(CurrentLevel().c_str(), tag, strnlen(tag, sizeof(tag)) != 0\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen()` is used instead of `strlen()`. `strnlen()` includes the null terminator in its count, which can help prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library is required for the `strncmp()` and `strlen()` functions.\n- `<string>`: This library is required for the `std::string` type and its `c_str()` method.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dcf28086-331c-3f4f-9f2a-5913f38e5870",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "df6a5e08-6dd9-3bd3-a67b-dbfd351c9711",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions due to data flow between them. There is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap. Additionally, the destination pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior, but the main risk here is the potential for overlapping memory regions. The source is not a string literal or constant, so the overlap risk is not reduced. This type of issue can lead to data corruption or crashes at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to safely handle overlapping memory regions.\n\n```cpp\n::memmove(data_ptr_, data, data_size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `data_ptr_` and `data` are valid, non-null pointers before calling the function. If there is a possibility that `data_ptr_` could be null, add a check before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (data_ptr_ != nullptr && data != nullptr) {\n    ::memmove(data_ptr_, data, data_size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3ba9d0f9-841d-315d-b0d1-526060516381",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not check for null termination. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the string is not properly null-terminated. In the provided code, `strlen(record->kernel_name) + 1` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `record->kernel_name` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that strings are properly null-terminated before using functions like `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that include built-in bounds checking, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the provided code could be:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t kernel_name_length = strnlen(record->kernel_name, MAX_LENGTH);\n(*pool)->Write(*record, record->kernel_name, kernel_name_length + 1,\n                           [](auto& record, const void* data) {\n                             record.kernel_name = static_cast<const char*>(data);\n                           })\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen` is used instead of `strlen`, and `MAX_LENGTH` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum expected length of `record->kernel_name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not explicitly include any library dependencies. However, the `strlen` function is part of the C Standard Library, so the code would require the `<cstring>` header in C++.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible at a later date."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3fb2c5a9-27e0-35d3-be6c-072547441667",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are not null, which further increases the risk. The classification specifically highlights undefined behavior due to possible overlap, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The source and destination expressions are not simple string literals or constant arrays, so the risk of overlap is credible. No guards or capacity checks are present to mitigate this risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\n::memmove(record_ptr_, &record, sizeof(Record));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if `record_ptr_` and `&record` refer to overlapping memory, the copy will be performed correctly without causing undefined behavior. Also, consider adding null pointer checks for both the source and destination before performing the copy to avoid null dereference issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a2d558fd-4fcc-32a9-9bb8-0e92164d75dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not properly handled. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using the `getenv` function. If it's necessary to use it, ensure that the returned value is not directly used without proper validation and sanitization. Also, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv` function from the C++ standard library, which is considered safer. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* env_var = std::getenv(\"ROCP_OUTPUT_DIR\");\nif (!env_var) {\n    // Handle the case when the environment variable is not set.\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d038861f-554d-32ab-96f6-5cec899e66ee",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or code execution vulnerabilities if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `getenv` function. If you need to use environment variables, consider using a safer alternative that does not have the same vulnerabilities. Validate all input and do not trust the data obtained from `getenv` function without validation. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer function. If you are using a POSIX system, you can use `secure_getenv` which returns NULL if the process is being run with elevated privileges. This can prevent potential privilege escalation attacks.\n\n```cpp\nchar *flush_str;\nflush_str = secure_getenv(\"ROCP_FLUSH_RATE\");\nif (flush_str == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `getenv` function is part of the C standard library (libc), so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "89399496-7edd-3189-bfcc-01980525cbee",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses insecure or deprecated C library functions. In this case, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security issues. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to potential security risks as the environment variables can be manipulated, leading to undefined behavior or security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv()` function directly. Instead, use secure alternatives that are less prone to manipulation. Validate all inputs and outputs to ensure they are as expected. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv()`, consider using a more secure alternative. For example, you can use a configuration file to store and retrieve your application settings. This way, you can control the access to this file and ensure that only authorized users can modify it.\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string get_config_value(const std::string& config_file, const std::string& key) {\n    std::ifstream file(config_file);\n    std::string line;\n    while (std::getline(file, line)) {\n        if (line.find(key) != std::string::npos) {\n            return line.substr(line.find('=') + 1);\n        }\n    }\n    return \"\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::string xml_name = get_config_value(\"config.txt\", \"ROCP_INPUT\");\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "54f85fd9-79ad-3668-ad9a-6a00742c7a95",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can be exploited if an attacker can control the environment in which your program runs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function directly in your code. If you need to use environment variables, consider safer alternatives such as `getenv_s` or use a wrapper function that validates the input and output of `getenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of directly using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like this:\n\n```cpp\nchar* plugin_name;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ROCTRACER_PLUGIN_LIB\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"ROCTRACER_PLUGIN_LIB doesn't exist!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nplugin_name = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\nif (!plugin_name)\n{\n    printf(\"Failed to allocate memory!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, plugin_name, requiredSize, \"ROCTRACER_PLUGIN_LIB\");\n```\n\nThis code first checks the size of the environment variable and then allocates the necessary memory for it. It also checks if the environment variable exists and if the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for `getenv_s`, `malloc`, and `exit`\n- `stdio.h` for `printf`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0154a3fd-5b67-3353-9d08-b79923f02348",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to the value in the environment, or NULL if there is no match. The returned string should not be modified by the program, but it can be overwritten by a subsequent call to `getenv()`, `setenv()`, or `unsetenv()`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other trusted libraries. In the case of `getenv()`, consider using a method that does not allow the environment to be changed during the execution of the program.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `getenv()` function with a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv()` function from the C++ Standard Library, which is thread-safe and does not allow the environment to be changed:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* trace_domain = std::getenv(\"ROCTRACER_DOMAIN\");\nif (!trace_domain) {\n    // Handle the case when the environment variable is not set\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This is a C++ Standard Library that provides general purpose functions, including the environment query function, memory management, and others.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9e26da27-41f4-35ec-973e-867380700979",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\nThe `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the specified environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. If the environment variable is found, it returns a pointer to the value of the environment variable, which can be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `getenv` function if possible. If you must use it, ensure that the returned value is not directly used without proper validation and sanitization. Never use the returned value as an array index, pointer, or loop counter. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative that allows for error handling or bounds checking. If you must use `getenv`, validate and sanitize the returned value before using it.\n\n```cpp\nchar *ctrl_str;\nctrl_str = getenv(\"ROCP_CTRL_RATE\");\nif (ctrl_str != NULL) {\n    // sanitize and validate ctrl_str before using it\n} else {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `getenv` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include `cstdlib` or `stdlib.h`.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "04b791c6-83f9-34e2-91f8-d4014adb07fb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or other security issues if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function directly. Instead, use secure alternatives that are provided by your platform or programming language. If you must use `getenv`, ensure that the returned value is not used directly without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cstring>\n\nchar *env_var;\nconst char *temp = std::getenv(\"ROCP_TRUNCATE_NAMES\");\nif(temp != nullptr) {\n    env_var = new char[std::strlen(temp)+1];\n    std::strcpy(env_var, temp);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the environment variable \"ROCP_TRUNCATE_NAMES\" exists. If it does, we allocate memory for `env_var` and then copy the value of the environment variable to `env_var`. This way, we avoid using the pointer returned by `getenv` directly.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- cstdlib\n- cstring\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8843731d-8f4a-3378-a18c-a216fb50f240",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. \n\nIn the given code snippet, `var != nullptr`, there is no prohibited C function being used. This is a simple null pointer check in C++, which is a good practice to prevent null pointer dereferencing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability, follow these general guidelines:\n\n- Avoid using dangerous C functions such as `strcpy()`, `strcat()`, `sprintf()`, and `gets()`. Instead, use safer alternatives like `strncpy()`, `strncat()`, `snprintf()`, and `fgets()`.\n- Always check the size of the buffer before writing data into it.\n- Use libraries that provide bounds checking.\n- Always initialize your variables.\n- Use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs mentioned earlier, the given code snippet `var != nullptr` is not a vulnerability. It's a good practice to check if a pointer is not null before dereferencing it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe given code snippet does not require any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "99f00c9b-7a50-3495-8572-78aceffb8eba",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to potential issues if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the language. In this case, consider using a method that does not involve environment variables to pass parameters to your program. If you must use environment variables, ensure that they are properly sanitized and validated before use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming you have a default buffer size\nconst int default_bufSize = 1024;\nchar* env_bufSize = getenv(\"ROCTRACER_BUFFER_SIZE\");\n\n// Check if the environment variable is set\nif(env_bufSize != NULL) {\n    // Convert the environment variable to an integer\n    int bufSize = atoi(env_bufSize);\n\n    // Validate the buffer size\n    if(bufSize <= 0) {\n        bufSize = default_bufSize;\n    }\n} else {\n    bufSize = default_bufSize;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the `cstdlib` library for the `getenv()` and `atoi()` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a2357cee-c047-38fb-87d7-cb1fd75b9296",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `std::atoi` function is used, which is a C library function that converts a string to an integer. This function does not perform any error checking, and if the string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero. This can lead to unexpected behavior if the string is not a valid integer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform error checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that are provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use `std::stoi` function which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::atoi` with `std::stoi` and add error handling:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* env_var = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nif (env_var) {\n    try {\n        if (std::stoi(env_var) != 0) {\n            // Do something\n        }\n    } catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n        // Handle error\n    } catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::getenv`\n- `<string>`: for `std::stoi`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "54b8ab6f-3b55-337e-a903-f4d446bc588d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "20ae3c66-29b3-37e1-9ea8-f96ee5c1099c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or even code execution if an attacker can control environment variables.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv()` function directly. If you need to use environment variables, consider using a safer alternative that does not have the same vulnerabilities. Validate all input and do not trust the data received from the environment variables blindly. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv()`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use a function that copies the environment variable into a buffer of a known size. This can prevent buffer overflow attacks. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nchar *value;\nsize_t len;\nerrno_t err = _dupenv_s(&value, &len, \"ROCTRACER_LOG\");\nif (err) {\n    printf(\"Error: %d\\n\", err);\n    var = NULL;\n} else {\n    var = value;\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for `getenv()`\n- `stdio.h` for `printf()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            }
